  "style": {
    "id": "tessellation-morph",
    "name": "Tessellation Morph",
    "description": "A wall of interlocking cut-paper tiles in two alternating colours, bevelled and layered so it is clearly 3D, where every tile is the same creature and the creature morphs smoothly (plain tile -> fish -> bird) without ever leaving a gap. Each lattice cell is a parallelogram whose opposite sides are one shared curve: what bulges out of one side is cut from the other, so any curve tiles. Key shapes are pairs of curves; the morph interpolates them point by point, and because each edge carries its own morph value the wall can show the whole transition in one frame, left to right. Eyes, gills, a beak and wing lines fade in only near a key shape. Tiles can leave the wall: a row of birds lifts off, folds along its body line to flap, and flies out, leaving bird-shaped holes. Whooshes, clicks and flutters come from the same morph plan.",
